> ### Gemini Workflow Assessment (2026-04-12)
> - Decision rule after direct re-check: do not ban Gemini outright, but stop treating it as a trusted primary implementation agent for repos you may ship, submit, or rely on later.
> - Fresh spot-checks during this review: `python -m pytest -q` PASS in `prognostic-meta` (`5 passed`), `tda-meta` (`3 passed`), `global-transportability-atlas` (`2 passed` at the time, now superseded by the 2026-04-21 atlas fix), `aleph-engine` (`4 passed`), and `retraction-gravity` (`6 passed`). `nma-quantum-shock` no longer fits the older â€œno pytest suiteâ€ note: the current local worktree now includes a suite and a read-only recheck on 2026-04-21 returned `30 passed in 176.11s`.
> - Pattern summary: Gemini output is strongest when the scope is narrow and the contract surface is small (`prognostic-meta`, `tda-meta`). Reliability drops as concept-stacking increases: `global-transportability-atlas` had a public-claims drift that was later repaired on 2026-04-21, `aleph-engine` is explicitly an omnibus speculative score, and `nma-quantum-shock` still reads more like a concept stress test than a validated NMA engine even though it now has a local pytest suite.
